1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:04,899
September first, nineteen seventy-three.

2
00:00:04,899 --> 00:00:13,606
Four hundred and eighty metres beneath the Atlantic, two men are
sitting in the dark inside a steel sphere that is running out of
air.

3
00:00:13,606 --> 00:00:15,165
Above them, the surface.

4
00:00:15,165 --> 00:00:18,024
Outside them, the weight of the whole ocean.

5
00:00:18,024 --> 00:00:24,976
When the hatch finally opens, the engineers will check the
cylinder and find twelve minutes of oxygen left.

6
00:00:24,976 --> 00:00:27,705
This is the deepest rescue ever completed.

7
00:00:27,705 --> 00:00:32,708
And it began with something so ordinary that nobody
on the ship looked twice.

8
00:00:32,708 --> 00:00:36,867
Three days earlier, the morning of August twenty-
ninth was calm.

9
00:00:36,867 --> 00:00:46,223
Two hundred and forty kilometres south-west of Cork, the cable
ship Vickers Voyager was laying a transatlantic telephone line
across the seabed.

10
00:00:46,223 --> 00:00:49,017
The work was slow and muddy and repetitive.

11
00:00:49,017 --> 00:00:57,204
A submersible crawls along the bottom at walking pace, blasting
jets of water into the ooze, then burying the cable behind it.

12
00:00:57,204 --> 00:00:59,803
Two men made that dive, again and again.

13
00:00:59,803 --> 00:01:01,622
Roger Chapman, twenty-eight.

14
00:01:01,622 --> 00:01:03,506
Roger Mallinson, thirty-five.

15
00:01:03,506 --> 00:01:04,741
Both professionals.

16
00:01:04,741 --> 00:01:07,210
Both, by that morning, a little tired.

17
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:10,877
That morning's dive was number three hundred and
twenty-five.

18
00:01:10,877 --> 00:01:14,412
Eight hours on the bottom, then up, then back to the
ship.

19
00:01:14,412 --> 00:01:21,968
At nine eighteen the submersible was already on the surface,
hanging from a crane, waiting for the towline to be clipped on.

20
00:01:21,968 --> 00:01:24,954
That is when the alarm sounded in the aft sphere.

21
00:01:24,954 --> 00:01:28,489
The towline had fouled the aft hatch and wrenched it
open.

22
00:01:28,489 --> 00:01:33,729
Water poured in, into a compartment that held machinery, oil,
and a tonne of buoyancy.

23
00:01:33,729 --> 00:01:37,508
Pisces Three heeled over, went upside down, and
began to fall.

24
00:01:37,508 --> 00:01:41,225
Inside, Chapman and Mallinson did the only things
they could.

25
00:01:41,225 --> 00:01:42,383
They cut the power.

26
00:01:42,383 --> 00:01:45,430
They released four hundred pounds of lead ballast.

27
00:01:45,430 --> 00:01:50,670
They switched off the depth gauge at five hundred feet, because
the glass might burst.

28
00:01:50,670 --> 00:01:57,130
Then they put cushions around their heads, so they would not
bite through their own tongues when they hit.

29
00:01:57,080 --> 00:01:59,883
Thirty seconds later, they hit the bottom.

30
00:01:59,883 --> 00:02:02,285
Four hundred and eighty metres down.

31
00:02:02,285 --> 00:02:07,423
The pressure outside was roughly forty-eight times
what it is at the surface.

32
00:02:07,423 --> 00:02:10,426
And inside, the arithmetic had already begun.

33
00:02:10,426 --> 00:02:14,430
A normal dive carries seventy-two hours of emergency
oxygen.

34
00:02:14,430 --> 00:02:16,499
Eight of those hours were gone.

35
00:02:16,499 --> 00:02:22,905
Sixty-four left, for two men, in a sphere six feet across, in
water four degrees above freezing.

36
00:02:22,905 --> 00:02:27,309
The aft sphere was flooded, so the craft sat tail-
down in the mud.

37
00:02:27,309 --> 00:02:32,113
Every system that drew power was off, which meant no
heat, and no light.

38
00:02:32,113 --> 00:02:35,717
They had one cheese sandwich, and one can of
lemonade.

39
00:02:35,717 --> 00:02:37,919
They did not open either of them.

40
00:02:37,919 --> 00:02:39,654
Not for seventy-six hours.

41
00:02:39,654 --> 00:02:46,060
That decision, and the carbon dioxide scrubbers they rationed by
hand, is the reason they lived.

42
00:02:46,010 --> 00:02:49,836
There is a detail in this story that most retellings
skip.

43
00:02:49,836 --> 00:02:52,805
Carbon dioxide does not only make you sleepy.

44
00:02:52,805 --> 00:02:54,124
It makes you stupid.

45
00:02:54,124 --> 00:02:57,291
The scrubber fan had to run every forty minutes.

46
00:02:57,291 --> 00:02:58,940
They let it run for less.

47
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:01,117
They spoke as little as possible.

48
00:03:01,117 --> 00:03:02,305
They barely moved.

49
00:03:02,305 --> 00:03:08,110
When the cold got into their bones, they squeezed each other's
hands instead of talking.

50
00:03:08,110 --> 00:03:11,805
The air went thick and manky, as Mallinson described
it.

51
00:03:11,805 --> 00:03:12,992
Their heads ached.

52
00:03:12,992 --> 00:03:15,829
Their sweat went cold inside their clothes.

53
00:03:15,829 --> 00:03:19,985
Through the porthole, for three days, there was
nothing to see.

54
00:03:19,985 --> 00:03:20,513
No fish.

55
00:03:20,513 --> 00:03:21,305
No daylight.

56
00:03:21,305 --> 00:03:25,923
Just silt, and the occasional scrape of a line
dragging over the hull.

57
00:03:25,923 --> 00:03:30,673
Twice they heard something, and let themselves
believe it was a rescuer.

58
00:03:30,673 --> 00:03:31,728
Twice it wasn't.

59
00:03:31,728 --> 00:03:36,214
On the surface, meanwhile, a very large machine was
being assembled.

60
00:03:36,214 --> 00:03:36,610
Badly.

61
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:39,177
The alarm went out across three navies.

62
00:03:39,177 --> 00:03:43,942
A Canadian coast guard ship, the John Cabot, turned
for the Celtic Sea.

63
00:03:43,942 --> 00:03:47,901
A British survey ship, HMS Hecate, came with special
ropes.

64
00:03:47,901 --> 00:03:55,886
From California, the United States Navy loaded a remote-
controlled recovery vehicle called CURV Three onto an aircraft.

65
00:03:55,886 --> 00:04:03,603
In between, the only craft that could actually reach them were
two sister submersibles, Pisces Two and Pisces Five.

66
00:04:03,603 --> 00:04:07,227
Pisces Two went down first, and lost its lifting
line.

67
00:04:07,227 --> 00:04:10,180
Pisces Five found them, and its hook failed.

68
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:12,864
Pisces Two came back, and took on water.

69
00:04:12,864 --> 00:04:16,085
CURV Three arrived, and had an electrical fault.

70
00:04:16,085 --> 00:04:19,843
The men at the bottom could hear the attempts
happening.

71
00:04:19,843 --> 00:04:21,453
They could not see them.

72
00:04:21,453 --> 00:04:29,640
By the end of the second night, Pisces Five was ordered up for
power, and they were alone again, with the air going stale.

73
00:04:29,590 --> 00:04:38,066
At four in the morning on September first, Pisces Two went down
for one last attempt, carrying a toggle designed for this exact
job.

74
00:04:38,066 --> 00:04:38,965
It clipped on.

75
00:04:38,965 --> 00:04:41,276
Then CURV Three fixed a second line.

76
00:04:41,276 --> 00:04:45,835
Only then did Chapman and Mallinson open the
sandwich and the lemonade.

77
00:04:45,835 --> 00:04:48,918
The lift began at ten fifty, and it was violent.

78
00:04:48,918 --> 00:04:55,531
A submersible with a flooded tail, swinging on lines, slamming
against itself for two and a half hours.

79
00:04:55,531 --> 00:04:59,833
At a hundred metres it stopped, so divers could add
heavier cables.

80
00:04:59,833 --> 00:05:04,071
At one seventeen in the afternoon, Pisces Three
broke the surface.

81
00:05:04,071 --> 00:05:05,420
The hatch was jammed.

82
00:05:05,420 --> 00:05:07,796
It took another half hour to open it.

83
00:05:07,796 --> 00:05:12,355
Chapman and Mallinson had been inside for eighty-
four and a half hours.

84
00:05:12,355 --> 00:05:16,528
When the cylinder was checked, twelve minutes of
oxygen remained.

85
00:05:16,528 --> 00:05:25,389
They had stretched seventy-two hours of life support by another
twelve and a half, using nothing but patience, arithmetic, and
each other.

86
00:05:25,389 --> 00:05:27,829
Both men lived for decades afterwards.

87
00:05:27,829 --> 00:05:30,783
They phoned one another every September first.

88
00:05:30,783 --> 00:05:33,994
And every year the ocean repeated the same lesson.

89
00:05:33,994 --> 00:05:35,535
Depth does not kill you.

90
00:05:35,535 --> 00:05:37,140
Running out of time does.

